    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-333300",
      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}{{field-2}},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the \n{{field-3}} \nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14124655/assets/8dc90697da27e52e0c1818d5529247d132944f30603936bedc191c4a14cb7d9c.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y={e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2.24$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $4.48$ | $1$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14124655/assets/3981c3c5bdfc66850798ee3c34031acb0d1cdfea7181eb34a0a0422b44cc2ef0.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14124655/assets/f2b5b95167b1ecd07aa1152aeac6dd454fbe71ba953b05c4da22d8c36f76277a.png)",
      "worked_solution": "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x\\text{-axis}.$ Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14124655/assets/8dc90697da27e52e0c1818d5529247d132944f30603936bedc191c4a14cb7d9c.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ y={e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2.24$ | $0$ | $0.11$ |\n| $y$ | $4.48$ | $1$ | $0.22$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14124655/assets/3981c3c5bdfc66850798ee3c34031acb0d1cdfea7181eb34a0a0422b44cc2ef0.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14124655/assets/f2b5b95167b1ecd07aa1152aeac6dd454fbe71ba953b05c4da22d8c36f76277a.png)",
